member.summary:
 Makes a C# method a node in the visual scripting palette. C# and the
 graphs are one system: a graph places the method like any engine node
 and calls it; the C# reaches back into the graph through
 ,  and
 .

     public class Turret : Script
     {
         // An action: execution in and out, and a Target - the object
         // whose Turret runs it (unwired: the graph's own object).
         [Node] public void Aim(Entity at) { ... }

         // A value: no execution pins, computed when something reads it.
         [Node(Pure = true)] public static float Lead(float speed, float distance) => distance / speed;
     }

 Parameters and the result may be bool, int, float, double, string,
 Vector2, Vector3, Vector4 or Entity. A parameter's C# default is the
 value its pin starts with.

 The base of every C# gameplay script. Put a C# Script component on an
 object, name the class, press Play:

     public class Spinner : Script
     {
         public float DegreesPerSecond = 90;
         public override void OnUpdate(float dt)
             => Rotation += new Vector3(0, DegreesPerSecond * dt, 0);
     }

 OnStart runs once when Play begins (or when the object is made while the
 game runs), OnUpdate every frame with the frame's seconds, OnStop when
 Play ends or the object is destroyed. What a script can do to its
 object and the world is the engine's own API - the same functions the
 visual scripting's nodes call, generated, so the two can never differ.
